package angelica:plugin@1.0.0;

/// Lens: the game's objects, read by path, the game's events watched, and
/// the game's actions taken for the player.
///
/// The game map describes the game's objects: their types and fields, the
/// roots where paths start, and the domain each field belongs to. A query
/// is a path from a root through fields, with filters on arrays and the
/// values to answer with:
///
/// ```text
/// inventory.containers[kind in (bag_1, bag_2)].items[item != 0] { item, quantity }
/// ```
///
/// `prepare` checks a query against the map for the running game build
/// and plans its reads; `run` reads the game's memory along the plan. Every
/// domain a query reads must be listed in the manifest's `[lens] read` and
/// approved by the player. The language is specified in
/// `docs/formats/plugin-api-v1.md`.
///
/// A watch is a query that starts at an event of the map instead of a
/// root, such as `action_used { kind, action }`: when the game reaches the
/// event's point, the host reads the query's rows from the call's arguments
/// and the objects they point to, and delivers them in the next frame as a
/// `watched` game event.
///
/// A field the map marks changeable, a value that exists on the player's
/// computer alone such as the camera's zoom limits or what the game's
/// windows show, is changed by `set` and put back by `restore` or when the
/// plugin stops.
///
/// An action is a function of the game the map describes, such as
/// `use_action`, which `act` calls for the player with named parameters, as
/// the game's own interface calls it.
///
/// Every function runs in the main instance, on the game's main thread
/// between frames; only `act` changes anything in the game. Jobs get
/// `wrong-context`.
interface lens {
    use types.{error};

    /// A row of one of the game's sheets.
    record sheet-row {
        sheet: string,
        row: u32,
    }

    /// The names of the set bits that have one, and every bit.
    record flag-set {
        names: list<string>,
        bits: u64,
    }

    variant value {
        boolean(bool),
        signed(s64),
        unsigned(u64),
        float(f64),
        /// Text as the game stores it, without its terminator.
        text(list<u8>),
        /// A number that is a row of a sheet, such as an item.
        sheet(sheet-row),
        /// A number that is one of the named values of an enum.
        named(string),
        %flags(flag-set),
    }

    /// One answer: the position in each array on the query's path, and a
    /// value per column, `none` where the game holds nothing: a pointer on
    /// its way is zero.
    record row {
        indexes: list<u32>,
        values: list<option<value>>,
    }

    record column {
        /// The name in the query's braces, or its alias.
        name: string,
        /// What the field holds, from the game map.
        doc: string,
    }

    record prepared {
        /// The handle `run` and `release` take.
        query: u32,
        columns: list<column>,
    }

    /// Checks and plans a query. A query that breaks the syntax or the
    /// model's rules returns `invalid-argument`; an unknown root or field,
    /// `not-found`; a domain the plugin may not read or a field no plugin
    /// reads, `permission-denied`; anything the running build lacks,
    /// `unavailable`; a plugin holding 256 queries, `limit-exceeded`. The
    /// message names the field.
    prepare: func(query: string) -> result<prepared, error>;

    /// The rows of a prepared query now: none while its root's object does
    /// not exist, such as before the player logs in. After the game map is
    /// reloaded, the query is planned again and returns `prepare`'s errors
    /// when it no longer fits. More than 4096 rows or 16 MiB of memory read
    /// returns `limit-exceeded`. An unknown handle returns `not-found`.
    run: func(query: u32) -> result<list<row>, error>;

    /// Checks and plans a watch: a query that starts at an event, with a
    /// filter on the event's arguments in brackets after its name, and
    /// starts it. The rows of each firing arrive in the next frame as a
    /// `watched` game event with the returned handle; a firing whose filter
    /// leaves everything out arrives as nothing. An event keeps at most its
    /// map's rate of firings per watch between two frames; more, and
    /// firings past 256 rows or 256 KiB of memory read, arrive counted as
    /// `watch-dropped`. Errors as for `prepare`; `unavailable` also when the
    /// host cannot hook the event's point in this build.
    watch: func(query: string) -> result<prepared, error>;

    /// Forgets a prepared query, and ends a watch or a rule.
    release: func(query: u32);

    /// Takes the map's action `action` for the player now, with each
    /// parameter by name; a parameter left out takes the map's default.
    /// An enum parameter takes `named`, a sheet row `sheet` or a number, a
    /// float `float` or a number, a bool `boolean`. The action's domain
    /// must be listed in the manifest's `[lens] act` and approved by the
    /// player. An unknown action returns `not-found`; a parameter that does
    /// not fit, `invalid-argument`; a domain the plugin may not act in, or
    /// acting while the player stopped plugins from acting,
    /// `permission-denied`; more often than the map's rate,
    /// `limit-exceeded`; a condition of the action that does not hold now
    /// (the message says which), something the build lacks, or the game
    /// refusing the action, `unavailable`.
    act: func(action: string, params: list<tuple<string, value>>) -> result<_, error>;

    /// Changes the value at `path`, a path without braces ending at a field
    /// the map marks changeable, such as `camera.camera.max_distance`, in
    /// every object the path reaches (at most 256), and returns how many:
    /// none while it reaches no object. The value takes the forms `act`'s
    /// parameters take, within the map's range. The field's domain must be
    /// listed in the manifest's `[lens] change` and approved; the domains on
    /// the way in `[lens] read` or `[lens] change`. A field the map does
    /// not mark changeable, or a domain the plugin may not change, returns
    /// `permission-denied`; a value that does not fit, `invalid-argument`;
    /// a value another plugin changed, or memory that cannot be written,
    /// `unavailable`; more than 64 paths changed, `limit-exceeded`. A text
    /// field takes `text`: UTF-8 shorter than the map's length, without
    /// control characters. The original is put back by `restore` and when
    /// the plugin stops, only where the path still reaches the same place
    /// and the value is still the one written. A field the map marks held,
    /// such as an element of a game window, the game sets again itself: the
    /// host applies the value again every frame wherever the path reaches
    /// until then.
    set: func(path: string, value: value) -> result<u32, error>;

    /// Puts back the originals of what `set` changed through `path`.
    restore: func(path: string) -> result<_, error>;

    /// Adds a rule at a decision point: an event of the map with outcomes,
    /// such as `chat_message_added`. `rule` is a query from the event
    /// without braces, with conditions in brackets and paths through its
    /// arguments as for `watch`; whenever it reaches anything, the host
    /// takes `outcome`, such as `hide`, itself: the game's function returns
    /// at once with the outcome's value and does not run. Rules apply in
    /// the order they were added, across plugins; the first that holds
    /// decides. Returns the handle `release` ends it with. The event's
    /// domain must be listed in the manifest's `[lens] change` and
    /// approved; domains on the rule's paths in `[lens] read` or
    /// `[lens] change`. An event without outcomes or a rule that breaks the
    /// syntax returns `invalid-argument`; an unknown event or outcome,
    /// `not-found`; a domain the plugin may not change, `permission-denied`;
    /// an event the build lacks or cannot hook, `unavailable`. Rules count
    /// toward the 256 held queries.
    decide: func(rule: string, outcome: string) -> result<u32, error>;
}
